{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ FIXED, RED→GREEN, and the fix follows a contract the codebase had already written down ═══\n\n**THE FIX (one line + a documented helper), `src/rebar/llm/runner.py`:**\n`run()` opened with `cfg = self._config`; it now opens with `cfg = _effective_config(self._config,\nreq)`, which returns the runner's config with `req.config.model` substituted when the request\ncarries a different one (and returns the floor object UNCHANGED otherwise).\n\n**WHY THIS SHAPE, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN INVENTION.** `structured_run.build_usage_limits` already\nstates the rule verbatim — \"`self._config` (`cfg`) is the floor; `req.config` is the per-call\noverride\" — added under bug 59bc precisely so a finding-rich Pass-2 verifier could raise its own\nstep budget \"without a shared runner's `self._config` changing under other steps\". `temperature`\nfollows the same rule at `runner.py:416`. The MODEL was the one per-call value that did not, so this\nis bringing an outlier into line with an existing documented contract rather than adding a new\nmechanism.\n\nTwo deliberate details:\n* **Substituted into a COPY of the config, not read as a loose variable at the resolution point.**\n  Everything model-adjacent downstream reads `cfg` — the `ProviderSession`, `capabilities_for`, the\n  fallback chain, `cache_settings_for`, `base_url` — so substituting into the config keeps all of\n  them consistent with the model that actually runs. Reading a separate `model` variable at\n  `_pai_model` only would have left the provider session built for the FLOOR's provider, which is\n  exactly the cross-provider incoherence this epic exists to remove.\n* **A copy, so honouring one step cannot change the floor for the next.** The runner instance is\n  shared across every step of a gate run; there is a test for this specific leak.\n\n**RED FIRST, CONFIRMED, AND FOR THE RIGHT REASON.** New `tests/unit/test_llm_model_per_request.py`\n(sibling to `test_llm_temperature.py`, the same per-call-override family) spies `_pai_model` — the\nresolution point, i.e. BENEATH where the value is handed in — and delegates to the real function so\nthe run completes. Pre-fix: 2 failed, 1 passed, both failures showing `anthropic:claude-opus-4-8`\n(the runner's own cfg) where the request asked for `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`. The passing one is\nthe CONTROL (request and runner agree ⇒ unchanged), so the failures cannot be \"no model reached the\nresolver\". Post-fix: 3/3 green. Third test pins the no-leak property: two sequential `run()` calls,\nthe first with an override, must resolve `[sonnet, opus]` — pre-fix it resolved `[opus, opus]`.\n\n**THE ORIGINAL REPORT, REPLICATED AS RESOLVED.** The same experiment that proved the defect now\nprints `model handed to _pai_model = ['PER-STEP-MODEL']` against `req.config.model =\n'PER-STEP-MODEL'`. Before the fix it printed `['CONSTRUCTOR-MODEL']`.\n\n**ON THE CAPTURE-POINT AC — stating this plainly rather than claiming the AC as written.** The plan\nasks for ONE offline end-to-end test observed RED pre-fix. What exists instead is a two-part guard,\nand the split is forced by where the defect turned out to be:\n * `tests/unit/workflow/test_code_review_step_model_attribution.py` — real gate doc, real\n   interpreter, real `RunnerAgentStep`, fake runner beneath it; asserts all five steps\n   (`base`/`round_a`/`round_b`/`verify`/`coach`) receive their declared class and that none falls\n   through to `cfg.model`. GREEN both before and after, because the upper half was never broken.\n   Mutation-checked (`step=None, workflow=None` ⇒ 5 failures naming all five steps), so it is\n   capable, not vacuous.\n * `tests/unit/test_llm_model_per_request.py` — the runner layer, RED pre-fix as above.\nComposed they cover the whole chain, but no single test yet observes five-steps-by-declaration\nBENEATH the runner. Building that one next (a real `PydanticAIRunner` with a `FunctionModel`\noverride driving the real gate, with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` enabled), because it is also what ACs 1-3\nneed — the usage-log attribution must be asserted from a log an actual gate run produced, not from\nreading `usage_log.py`. One harness serves both.\n\n**AND A HONEST NOTE ON THE FIRST [operator-attested] CRITERION.** It asks for the live probe to be\nRE-RUN on the broken code so the per-step attribution names the seam. That did not happen and now\ncannot: the seam was named by a deterministic local experiment (spy `_pai_model`, compare against\n`req.config.model`) that is strictly stronger than a live probe — it isolates the mechanism, costs\nno tokens, and is replayable — and the code is now fixed. Step 0's attribution IS built and is what\nthe new end-to-end test asserts against. Flagging for the operator rather than quietly treating the\ncriterion as met by a different artifact.\n\n**SCOPE NOTE for the record:** the fix landed in `runner.py`, which is in this ticket's Scope and\n`file_impact` but was described there as the `usage_log.record` call site; the Scope's \"expect to\nchange ONE of these\" list (gate_dispatch / runs / batch_runner) named three files and the answer was\nin none of them. The plan anticipated the uncertainty (\"the seam is NOT yet identified, and Step 0\nexists to identify it\") but its enumeration was a prediction that the data falsified. Recording the\ndivergence rather than letting the plan read as if it had called it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785622756119239001,"uuid":"b0d1a1fe-feeb-4fbf-be39-e69b4c91c6a7"}